    d: 'Work sampling is a statistical technique that uses many random, instantaneous observations to estimate the percentage of time spent on different activities (working, waiting, idle, etc.).',
    why: 'It measures long or irregular activities (like maintenance or material handling) cheaply, without continuous stopwatch observation.',
    when: 'For non-repetitive work, measuring idle time and utilization of people or machines, and setting allowances.',
    steps: ['Define activity categories (working, waiting for material, walking, idle, etc.).', 'Decide number of observations needed for required accuracy.', 'Observe at random times and record the category.', 'Calculate % of observations in each category.', 'Act on the largest non-value-adding categories.'],
    ex: '400 observations of forklift drivers: 52% moving loads, 20% empty travel, 18% waiting, 10% idle.',
    hyd: '300 random observations of the welding cell: 48% arc-on, 22% loading/unloading, 14% waiting for tubes, 10% fixture adjustment, 6% idle. Waiting for tubes is attacked with a supermarket.',
    impl: ['Use random time tables.', 'Inform people of the purpose to avoid behaviour change.'],
    f: { expr: 'Activity % = Observations in category ÷ Total observations × 100; required n = 4p(1−p) ÷ L² (95% conf.)', vars: ['p = estimated proportion', 'L = desired accuracy (absolute)'], worked: 'p = 0.2, L = 0.04 → n = 4 × 0.2 × 0.8 ÷ 0.0016 = 400 observations.' },
    cq: ['Of 250 observations, 60 showed "waiting". Waiting %?', '24%', ['60%', '4.2%', '19%'], '60 ÷ 250 × 100 = 24%.'],
    mis: ['Observing at fixed times (not random).', 'Too few observations.'],
    beg: 'Take many quick snapshots to see how time is really spent.',
    mgr: 'A manager uses work sampling to reveal invisible losses — such as waiting for cranes or material — that never appear in production reports.',
    adv: ['Low cost', 'Good for irregular work'],
    lim: ['Does not give detailed element times'],
    rel: ['Time and Motion Study', 'Work Measurement', 'Machine Utilization'],
